WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.750
Alaska's North Slope:
the Arctic coastal plain,

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:05.710
north of the Brooks Range.

3
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.830
Permafrost underlies about
85 per cent of Alaska.

4
00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:17.850
Arctic and boreal permafrost holds 1,460
to 1,600 gigatonnes of organic carbon:

5
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.650
almost twice the carbon in the atmosphere.

6
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.110
The plants above store far less.

7
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:28.010
But NOAA reports the tundra region has
shifted from a carbon dioxide sink to a

8
00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:29.310
small source.

9
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:30.350
Why?

10
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:31.710
Are plants dying?

11
00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:33.510
Is melting sea ice to blame?

12
00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:34.670
Oil wells?

13
00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:38.430
In this mission, you'll size
up the tundra's carbon stores.

14
00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:41.230
Then you'll follow carbon
in and out through the year,

15
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.710
and test each explanation.

16
00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.850
Your mission: explain why the Arctic
tundra is giving back some of its carbon.
